**Remark 4.4 (Comparison with the whole space).** For $3\nmid d$ both sectors have extrinsic term $4/(d+3)$, which exceeds the intrinsic term $3/(d+2)$ of $\mathbb C^d$ itself, because $4(d+2)-3(d+3)=d-1>0$. By Theorem 1 of Sec. IV of arXiv:2512.23013v1, the average of $\Delta M(\mathcal E)$ over random subspaces of fixed dimension $k$ equals $\mathbb E_U[M_B]-\mathbb E_U[M_S]$; combined with Eq. (III.10)–(III.11) and cited fact (c), this says that the random-subspace average of the extrinsic term is $3/(d+2)$ for odd $d$. Hence for $3\nmid d$ both parity sectors have extrinsic term above the random-subspace average, that is, gap below the random-subspace average gap $I(k)-3/(d+2)$ for the same $k$. Stance: repo-derived (the averaging statement is the cited Theorem 1).

